# Personal / machine-local overrides loaded by the Makefile via
# `-include .env.local; export`. Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in
# the values you need. `.env.local` is gitignored — never commit it.
#
# IMPORTANT — quoting:
#   The Makefile parses this file as Makefile syntax (NOT bash). Everything
#   after the `=` is the LITERAL value — do NOT wrap values in quotes; the
#   quotes become part of the string. Embedded `|`, apostrophes, spaces, etc.
#   are fine as-is.
#
# Example (correct):
#   EMAILOPS_PERSONAL_ACCOUNT=you@example.com
# Example (wrong — value becomes the literal 19-byte string "'you@example.com'"):
#   EMAILOPS_PERSONAL_ACCOUNT='you@example.com'

# ── App data dir ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where `make dev` / `make demo` / `make cli-kv-personal` find the app data.
# Defaults to a repo-local directory when unset. Point this at the prod app
# data dir to share state with the running app (the cli-kv-personal bench
# reuses this var so a single override does double duty).
#   `?=` means "set if not already set" — leaves it overridable from the CLI.
# EMAILOPS_DATA_DIR ?= $(HOME)/Library/Application Support/com.emailops.app

# ── make cli-kv-personal ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Account email registered in $EMAILOPS_DATA_DIR (any row in the `accounts`
# table). The prompt itself is hardcoded in scripts/cli_kv_personal.sh.
# EMAILOPS_PERSONAL_ACCOUNT=you@example.com

# ── Apple signing ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# See .env.signing.example for the release-build credentials. Those vars live
# in a dedicated file because `make build-mac` sources it explicitly via bash
# (single-quote semantics differ from Makefile-include parsing).
